\title{The First Cohomology of a Cyclic Figure:\\ A Homological Theory of Impossible Objects}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{\today}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
Impossible figures --- the Penrose triangle, Escher's endless staircase, the
one-sided M\"obius band --- are usually treated as curiosities of visual
perception. We develop them instead as objects of algebraic topology. A cyclic
figure is modelled as a discrete arrangement of $n$ overlapping patches carrying
local \emph{depth increments} valued in an abelian group $A$. We organise this
local data into a two-term cochain complex whose maps are the \emph{coboundary}
(the increment forced by a global height assignment) and the \emph{holonomy} (the
total increment accumulated once around the loop). Our main results are: (i) both
maps are group homomorphisms; (ii) holonomy is surjective; (iii) the complex is
\emph{exact} --- the coboundaries coincide exactly with the figures of vanishing
holonomy; and consequently (iv) the first cohomology group of a cyclic figure is
\emph{canonically isomorphic to the coefficient group}, $H^1 \cong A$, with
holonomy realising the isomorphism. We deduce that a figure is realizable if and
only if its holonomy vanishes, that holonomy is a complete invariant of the
cohomology class, that the Penrose triangle is impossible and generates the
integral impossibility group, and that a band with an odd number of orientation
flips is non-orientable because it carries the nonzero class of
$H^1(S^1;\Z/2)$. The development is a discrete, combinatorial analogue of the
classical computation $H^1(S^1;A) \cong A$, and it turns the informal notion of
an ``impossible object'' into a precise, computable topological obstruction.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

An impossible figure is a two-dimensional drawing that the eye reads as a
coherent three-dimensional object, even though no such object exists. The
Penrose triangle presents three mutually perpendicular beams, each junction
locally consistent, whose global assembly cannot be realised in Euclidean space.
Escher's \emph{Ascending and Descending} depicts a rectangular staircase on which
every flight rises yet the loop returns to its starting height. \emph{Waterfall}
routes a stream downhill along a closed circuit.

The unifying feature is a conflict between \textbf{local} consistency and
\textbf{global} consistency. Each junction or overlap of the figure is
individually plausible; the contradiction appears only when one traverses a
closed loop and demands that the accumulated data return to its starting value.
This is precisely the situation cohomology was invented to measure: the failure
of locally consistent data to patch into a global section.

This paper makes that analogy exact for cyclic (loop-like) figures. We define a
cochain complex directly from the combinatorial data of the figure, identify its
coboundary and holonomy maps, and prove the complex is exact. The payoff is a
complete classification: the group of impossibility classes of a cyclic figure is
canonically isomorphic to the coefficient group, and holonomy is the
isomorphism. Every downstream fact --- the impossibility of the Penrose
triangle, the non-orientability of the M\"obius band and Klein bottle, the ``only
one impossible triangle up to strength'' phenomenon --- follows as a corollary.

Throughout, $n \geq 1$ is the number of patches, and $A$ is an arbitrary abelian
group written additively. We index patches by the cyclic group $\Z/n$, so that
patch $i$ overlaps patch $i+1$ with indices read modulo $n$.

\section{Definitions}

\begin{definition}[Cochain / figure]
A \emph{cyclic figure} on $n$ patches with coefficients in $A$ is a function
$t : \Z/n \to A$, also called a $1$-cochain. The value $t_i$ is the \emph{local
increment} across the overlap between patch $i$ and patch $i+1$. We write $C^1 =
(\Z/n \to A)$ for the group of all figures under pointwise addition.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Gauge / coboundary]
A \emph{gauge} is a function $h : \Z/n \to A$ assigning a global value to each
patch. Its \emph{coboundary} is the figure
\[
(\delta h)_i \;=\; h_{i+1} - h_i, \qquad i \in \Z/n.
\]
We write $C^0 = (\Z/n \to A)$ for the group of gauges. A figure $t = \delta h$ is
called \emph{realizable}.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Holonomy]
The \emph{holonomy} of a figure $t$ is
\[
\hol(t) \;=\; \sum_{i \in \Z/n} t_i \;\in\; A.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Cohomologous figures / first cohomology]
Two figures $t,s$ are \emph{cohomologous}, written $t \sim s$, if $t - s = \delta
h$ for some gauge $h$. This is an equivalence relation, and the quotient group
$H^1 = C^1/\im(\delta)$ is the \emph{first cohomology group} of the cyclic
figure. A cohomology class is the \emph{impossibility class} of a figure.
\end{definition}

These definitions assemble into the two-term cochain complex
\begin{equation}\label{eq:complex}
C^0 \xrightarrow{\ \delta\ } C^1 \xrightarrow{\ \hol\ } A.
\end{equation}

\section{Main results}

\subsection{The maps are homomorphisms}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:hom}
The coboundary $\delta : C^0 \to C^1$ and the holonomy $\hol : C^1 \to A$ are
group homomorphisms; in particular $\hol(t - s) = \hol(t) - \hol(s)$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Both are built from addition. For $\delta$, $(\delta(h+g))_i = (h_{i+1}-h_i) +
(g_{i+1}-g_i)$ and $\delta 0 = 0$. For $\hol$, $\hol(t+s) = \sum_i (t_i + s_i) =
\sum_i t_i + \sum_i s_i$ and $\hol(0)=0$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Holonomy is surjective}

\begin{proposition}[Surjectivity]\label{prop:surj}
For every $a \in A$ there is a figure $t$ with $\hol(t) = a$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Define $t_0 = a$ and $t_i = 0$ for $i \neq 0$. Then $\hol(t) = \sum_i t_i = a$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Exactness}

\begin{lemma}[Coboundaries are closed]\label{lem:closed}
For every gauge $h$, $\hol(\delta h) = 0$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$\hol(\delta h) = \sum_i (h_{i+1} - h_i) = \sum_i h_{i+1} - \sum_i h_i$. The map
$i \mapsto i+1$ is a bijection of $\Z/n$, so the two sums are equal and their
difference vanishes.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Discrete Poincar\'e lemma]\label{lem:poincare}
If $\hol(t) = 0$, then $t = \delta h$ for the partial-sum gauge $h_i =
\sum_{j=0}^{i-1} t_j$ (with $h_0 = 0$).
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
For each non-seam index, $h_{i+1} - h_i = t_i$ by definition of partial sums. At
the wrap-around seam the required identity $h_0 - h_{n-1} = t_{n-1}$ reduces to
$\sum_{j=0}^{n-1} t_j = 0$, which is the hypothesis $\hol(t) = 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exactness]\label{thm:exact}
The complex \eqref{eq:complex} is exact at $C^1$: $\im(\delta) = \ker(\hol)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$\im(\delta) \subseteq \ker(\hol)$ is Lemma~\ref{lem:closed}; the reverse
inclusion is Lemma~\ref{lem:poincare}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Realizability criterion]\label{thm:realiz}
A figure $t$ is realizable if and only if $\hol(t) = 0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Immediate from Theorem~\ref{thm:exact}: realizable means $t \in \im(\delta)$,
zero holonomy means $t \in \ker(\hol)$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Classification: $H^1 \cong A$}

\begin{theorem}[Complete invariant]\label{thm:invariant}
$t \sim s$ if and only if $\hol(t) = \hol(s)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $t \sim s$ then $t-s = \delta h$, so $\hol(t)-\hol(s) = \hol(\delta h) = 0$ by
Lemma~\ref{lem:closed}. Conversely $\hol(t)=\hol(s)$ gives $\hol(t-s)=0$, so
$t-s=\delta h$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:poincare}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[First cohomology of a cyclic figure]\label{cor:H1}
Holonomy induces an isomorphism $H^1 \xrightarrow{\ \cong\ } A$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
By Proposition~\ref{prop:hom} holonomy is a homomorphism $C^1 \to A$; by
Theorem~\ref{thm:exact} its kernel is $\im(\delta)$, so it descends to an
injection $H^1 \to A$. By Proposition~\ref{prop:surj} it is surjective.
\end{proof}

This is the discrete counterpart of $H^1(S^1;A) \cong A$: the cyclic arrangement
of overlapping patches is a combinatorial circle whose first cohomology is free
of rank one over $A$, with holonomy as coordinate.

\subsection{The Penrose triangle}

\begin{theorem}[Penrose impossibility and generation]\label{thm:penrose}
Over $A = \Z$ with $n = 3$, the figure $p$ with $p_0 = 1$, $p_1 = p_2 = 0$ has
$\hol(p) = 1 \neq 0$, so the Penrose triangle is not realizable. Moreover $[p]
\in H^1 \cong \Z$ is a generator, so every integral impossibility class is an
integer multiple $k[p]$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$\hol(p)=1\neq 0$ gives non-realizability by Theorem~\ref{thm:realiz}. By
Corollary~\ref{cor:H1}, $[p] \mapsto 1$ generates $\Z$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Orientation, the M\"obius band and the Klein bottle}

Taking $A = \Z/2$ reinterprets $t_i$ as an orientation flip: $t_i = 1$ if
handedness reverses across overlap $i$, else $0$. Holonomy is the parity of the
number of flips.

\begin{theorem}[Orientation obstruction]\label{thm:orient}
A cyclic band with an odd number of orientation flips has holonomy equal to the
nonzero element of $\Z/2$; its class in $H^1(S^1;\Z/2) \cong \Z/2$ is nonzero, so
it admits no consistent global orientation and is non-orientable.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The number of flips is odd iff its residue is $1$, i.e. $\hol(t) = 1 \neq 0$ in
$\Z/2$; by Theorem~\ref{thm:realiz} no realizing gauge exists.
\end{proof}

Gluing two M\"obius bands yields a Klein bottle, whose non-orientability is again
the odd-flip holonomy class. The Escher staircase (holonomy in $\Z$ or $\R$) and
the M\"obius band (holonomy in $\Z/2$) are two instances of one theorem,
differing only in the coefficient group.

\section{Algorithms}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Holonomy / realizability test.} Compute $\hol(t) = \sum_i t_i$ in
$O(n)$ additions; $t$ is realizable iff the result is $0$.
\item \textbf{Gauge reconstruction.} Given $\hol(t) = 0$, set $h_0 = 0$, $h_i =
h_{i-1} + t_{i-1}$; then $\delta h = t$. $O(n)$.
\item \textbf{Cohomology comparison.} $t \sim s$ iff $\hol(t) = \hol(s)$;
compare in $O(n)$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:invariant}).
\end{itemize}

\section{Applications and discussion}

The complex \eqref{eq:complex} is precisely the abelian lattice gauge theory on a
cycle: gauges are $0$-cochains, figures are connections, holonomy is the Wilson
loop, and cohomology is gauge equivalence. The isomorphism $H^1 \cong A$ says the
Wilson loop is the complete gauge-invariant observable. The $\Z/2$ specialisation
gives a combinatorial proof that odd-twist bands are non-orientable, packaging
the first Stiefel--Whitney obstruction as a holonomy. In perception, integrating
local depth cues around detected contours yields a principled detector for
impossible figures: nonzero holonomy flags the illusion.

The homological packaging replaces a case-by-case list of impossible figures with
one structural theorem. Surjectivity says every impossibility is attainable;
exactness says holonomy is the \emph{only} obstruction to realizability; the
isomorphism says holonomy is a complete invariant, indexing figures faithfully by
$A$.

\section{Future directions}

This cycle upgraded the pointwise ``realizable iff zero holonomy'' dichotomy to a
homological statement: the coboundary and holonomy maps form an exact two-term
complex, and the first cohomology group of a cyclic figure is canonically
isomorphic to the coefficient group. The theory further extends to
two-dimensional figures (Escher's \emph{Waterfall}) on the discrete torus, where
realizability is governed by a local curvature and two global periods together
with a discrete Stokes / Gauss--Bonnet identity.

Three conjectures push the program further. \emph{Higher-genus impossibility:} for
a cyclic figure on a closed orientable surface of genus $g$, the impossibility
group should be free of rank $2g$, matching $H^1(S^1) \cong A$ (rank $1$) and
$H^1(T^2) \cong A^2$ (rank $2$). \emph{Non-abelian monodromy:} with gluing data in
a non-abelian group $G$, realizability should be governed by an ordered monodromy,
turning the holonomy invariant into a conjugacy class. \emph{Torsion and
orientation:} on non-orientable bases the impossibility group carries a
distinguished $2$-torsion class detecting one-sidedness.

\section{Conclusion}

Modelling a cyclic figure as a $1$-cochain valued in an abelian group, we proved
that the coboundary and holonomy maps form an exact complex, that holonomy is
surjective, and hence that the first cohomology group is canonically isomorphic to
the coefficient group. Realizability is equivalent to vanishing holonomy;
holonomy is a complete invariant of impossibility; the Penrose triangle generates
the integral theory; and orientation flips over $\Z/2$ recover the
non-orientability of the M\"obius band and Klein bottle. The impossibility of an
object is, quite literally, a number one computes by walking around it.

\end{document}

